4:30 PM SATURDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...West Elk Mountains, Elk Mountains, San Juan Mountains, and\nLa Plata Mountains\n*WHEN...From 4:30 PM Saturday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS...Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous\navalanches large enough to bury a human.\n\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...A potent winter storm increases\navalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause\nnatural avalanches above treeline. Check the forecast at\nwww.colorado.gov/avalanche.\n\nBeyer & Pritchett","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"West Elk and Sawatch Mountains; Northwestern San Juan Mountains; Southwest San Juan Mountains; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.5d9a0ec4f46df7deb3c2c0e5015df58147db243f.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 31 at 10:37PM MDT until November 2 at 11:00AM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, San\nJuan River Basin and Southwest San Juan Mountains and southeast\nUtah, including the following areas, Canyonlands/Natural Bridges,\nLa Sal and Abajo Mountains and Southeast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...From 5 AM MST Sunday through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recently burned areas, and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- 1 to 3 inches of rain is expected across portions of southeast\nUtah and southwest Colorado from Sunday morning through Monday\nm